      "math_academy_id": "q-48398",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2]?$",
      "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ and differentiable on $(-1,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.5.$   Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-1,2).$  Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
